no \verb{CASE DEFAULT}, so they would be silently parsed and dropped as
no-ops rather than doing anything, and so are not written here. In
particular, height-above is triggered by requesting \code{out_elev}/
\code{out_depth}/\code{out_elev_bil}/\code{out_depth_bil}, not by a
"calculate height above" switch. See this package's \code{CLAUDE.md} for that
finding.

The \verb{ATTRIBUTE LIST} block is read by a separate, shared \code{ReadList()}
routine (\verb{..\\\\modules\\\\Utilities.f90}) that rewinds the input file and
rescans it from the top looking specifically for that keyword. So, unlike
the "must come last" constraint documented for \code{\link[=RIL_input]{RIL_input()}} and
\code{\link[=bldgrds_input]{bldgrds_input()}}, its position in the file does not actually matter here
-- confirmed against \code{ReadList()}'s source, not just inferred -- because
\code{ValleyFloor.f90}'s own main keyword loop also has no \verb{CASE DEFAULT} and so
just ignores the block's lines as it passes over them on its way to
whatever keyword comes next. This writes it in the same position as the
reference file anyway: after the main keywords, before
\verb{HEIGHT ABOVE STEP LIST}/\verb{VALLEY WIDTH WINDOW IN CHANNEL WIDTHS}.
